
=== Comparison with ZZT and Super ZZT ===

Super ZZ Zero is a ZZT-like, but with many differences. (There are actually
a lot more enhancements than described here.)

In ZZT, a board is limited to 150 stats, one of which is the player. Each
stat can have its own text, and some other parameters, and only stats can
have other stuff underneath since they are stored with stat data. But, in
Super ZZ Zero, each stat has zero or more instances; you can create and
destroy instances at run time (even if there are initially no instances)
but not create and destroy stats. Each instance of a stat has its own
instruction pointer, lock flag, and facing direction.

Super ZZ Zero has a separate under layer and main layer, so the stuff
underneath other tiles can be remembered even if they aren't stats. In
addition, each tile on a layer has kind/colour/parameter, unlike only
kind/colour of ZZT. This means that you do not need many different text
elements, since the parameter can be used for specifying the character
of the text, rather than using the colour for this purpose. Parameters
can also be used for many things that would require stats in ZZT.

Probably the most significant feature of Super ZZ Zero is that you can
define your own elements (actually, you must define your own elements,
although a template can be used if you do not want to define your own;
the default template has many elements like ZZT has, and some others).
You can also define many global behaviours in a similar way.

Boards can be of different sizes, and can also be scrollable.

Dark rooms are possible like in ZZT, and you can customize the shape of
the torch light (and this can change during the game). Boards also have
an overlay (like MegaZeux; see the below section), and it can be disabled
or enabled at run time, so that the darkness of rooms can also be changed
at run time.

The screen layout can be customized; this includes the viewport (and how
the scrolling works), the message line, and the display of counters such
as health, money, etc. You can also define separate screen layouts for
text windows, and different boards may have different screen layouts if
wanted (although usually the share the same screen layouts).

Scripts are like ZZT-OOP, but have many new features, e.g. #DIEITEM (moves
the player where the object is when it dies), #ZAP can zap choices as well
as labels, #ZAPALL, #ESCAPE (allows variable data in text to be displayed),
subroutines, numeric expressions, and more.

However, there is also separate synchronous program code which applies to
the entire world and does not use scripts; this is a different programming
language, which is compiled into P-codes, and can be used for many other
things, including defining new script commands.

The #PLAY command (and SFX, in synchronous codes) has a different syntax
than ZZT, but has additional features such as overtone and undertone
scales, sixty-fourth notes, priority numbers, and some additional
percussions than ZZT has.

In ZZT, sometimes external .HLP files can be displayed, and some worlds use
this feature. In Super ZZ Zero, these .HLP files are included inside of the
world file, so you do not need additional files than the world file.

Whether or not boards persist when you leave and re-enter can be set per
board individually, instead of automatically persisting all boards.


=== Comparison with MegaZeux ===

There are many features like MegaZeux has, and some that MegaZeux does not
have, but also some Super ZZ Zero does not have but might or might not be
added in future.

Everything needed for a world to work is stored in the world file in Super
ZZ Zero, unlike in MegaZeux where separate files are used. Some of these
features (e.g. music files) are not implemented yet, but when (if) they are
implemented (some of these are planned), then they will store the data in
the world file instead of in separate files.

In Super ZZ Zero, you can define your own kind of elements (and must do so
if you do not use the template), unlike MegaZeux where the set of possible
kind of pieces is fixed and you cannot change them (you must use Robotic
instead). Super ZZ Zero has scriptable objects too like MegaZeux has, but
this is separate from defining new kind of pieces.

One feature of Super ZZ Zero is screen definitions. A board can be
associated with a screen definition, and screens are usually shared between
multiple boards but you can also make different boards use different screen
definitions. Text windows can also use screen definitions.

In MegaZeux, you cannot customize much about the viewport other than its
size and colour. In Super ZZ Zero, the viewport can be defined as a part
of the screen definition; however, you cannot change the viewport size at
run time in Super ZZ Zero, unlike MegaZeux (you can still use a different
viewport size (and shape) for different screens though).

MegaZeux has board overlays, which can be made static or not static, but
not both at the same time. Super ZZ Zero does not have static overlays but
you can define screen layouts as well as overlays, and these can be used at
the same time; screen layouts can be used for many of the things that
static overlays were previously used for (such as HUD).

In MegaZeux, there is a menu opened by pushing enter, which displays
health and other stuff, and some other options. In Super ZZ Zero, F1 is
used for options, but stuff like health and counters are defined by the
specific game world instead, and can be displayed during the game instead
of needing a separate menu. The screen layout can include components such
as the values of numeric counters (there are several ways these can be
displayed, including as decimal numbers, meters, Lsd money, and more),
which makes it easy to make screen layouts.

Super ZZ Zero does have stats, but they do not work like in ZZT. Each
stat can have a script (like a Robotic program, but the syntax is more
like ZZT), but also has zero or more instances, which can be added and
removed at run time (even if there are initially no instances). Anything
that needs to store more data than the kind/colour/parameter, or that
needs to be updated in stat order instead of board scanning, will use
stats; many other things will not need stats.

MegaZeux Robotic code has some klugy stuff due to how it changed over time
and kept the compatible format, but Super ZZ Zero uses syntax for numeric
expressions, subroutines, etc which are better than MegaZeux in my opinion.

Super ZZ Zero does have a mechanism for sensors like MegaZeux has, but it
is a bit different (and there is a bit more customizability). You can have
multiple element definitions which work like sensors, and they can be
programmed to allow any stats to move on them and not necessarily only the
player (this is fully customizable). Different kind of sensors can also be
made pushable or not pushable, and other properties.

The overlay in Super ZZ Zero does not have the feature of MegaZeux that
character code 32 is transparent; instead, being transparent is a separate
bit. There are other additional bits stored in overlay cells as well,
including translucent backgrounds, user-defined bits, and solid. Stuff in
the overlay can be moved around (if the specific game is programmed to do
this), which means solid is meaningful; stats can also be in the overlay.

In MegaZeux, the label name must specify whether or not it is a subroutine
and anything that calls it must know if it is a subroutine or not. Super
ZZ Zero uses a separate specification of if a label is a subroutine, and
callers that send a message to it do not need to be aware of that. This
means that messages do not need to have a separate subroutine message.

Super ZZ Zero does not have (nearly) unlimited named counters like MegaZeux
has. There is up to sixteen named flags (like ZZT), and you can store more
stuff in program memory and in global status variables (of which you can
define up to sixteen). There are other places to store data as well, such
as in inventory lists (which can be used to make keys like MegaZeux).

Whether or not boards persist when you leave and re-enter can be set per
board individually, instead of automatically persisting all boards. This
can also be changed at run time. (New versions of MegaZeux now have the
ability to automatically reset boards, which is similar to making them
not persist.)

Some of the feature requests for MegaZeux are already implemented in Super
ZZ Zero, such as:

* Remapping joypad buttons/hats/axes.

* Custom terrains, items, creatures, etc. (This also makes possible some
of the other feature requests for MegaZeux in Super ZZ Zero.)

* Board shot limit. (This is implemented in the default template, and not
as a part of Super ZZ Zero itself.)

* Board editor find/replace. (The implementation in Super ZZ Zero is
actually more general.)

* Board list introspection: The only part which is implemented in Super ZZ
Zero is determining the name of boards with specific board numbers; you
cannot determine anything else about boards other than the current board.

* Manipulation of text boxes.

* Check blocked status at exact position.

* Centipedes (implemented in the default template; not built-in).

* ZZT-like side bar, outside of the viewport and separate from overlays.

Some feature requests (and some features that are already implemented in
MegaZeux) are deliberately not implemented in Super ZZ Zero, such as:

* External file access. (You cannot use external files for fonts, palettes,
music, sound effects, etc either; all of this stuff can be included in the
world file. However, the contents of text windows and message scrollback
can be manually sent to the printer or to a file by the end user; the game
author cannot configure these things.)

* IF MUSICON and IF PCSFXON. (The audio state can never affect the
behaviour of the game in Super ZZ Zero.)

* WAIT PLAY. (The audio state can never affect the behaviour of the game
in Super ZZ Zero. However, in future there might be added the possibility
to suspend the entire game until a sound finishes playing, but it will
still be possible for the user to interrupt this and continue immediately.)

* The game speed (like the MZX_SPEED counter). The user can always change
this manually at run time and can set it in the user configuration file.
The game world can set a recommended initial speed, although this can be
overridden by the end user.

* Random number seeds (RANDOM_SEED0 and RANDOM_SEED1): It is currently
possible to set these in the debugger, but not by the game world. There
is also no guarantee that the same algorithm will be used for random
numbers in later versions of Super ZZ Zero, so being able to change the
random number seed is not helpful. (Implement your own random number
generator if you need consistent output.)

* Date/time access is deliberately not available; the game is not supposed
to depend on the current date/time.

* The set of key codes available to game worlds is deliberately limited.
(For example, function keys are always used for system functions (such as
option menu, message scrollback, save/restore game, etc).) (In future, a
few more might be added to te set of key codes available to game worlds,
such as home, end, page up, and page down.)

* Saving the game automatically by the game world is not available. The
game state must be saved by the end user pushing the F3 or F5 key.

* You cannot block restoring save games. It is always possible to restore
a save game (although you can prevent saving the game).


=== Comparison with ZZ Zero ===

It is similar in many ways, although there are many differences. Super ZZ
Zero is an entirely new program and does not use any parts of ZZ Zero.

* Program memory size is now 64K instead of 32K. (This is in cells, not in
bytes; the number of bytes is 128K.)

* Registers are now 32-bits (instead of 16-bits). (Program memory cells
are still only 16-bits, though.)

* Multiple screen layouts can be defined instead of only one.

* Boards can now be bigger than 80x25 (as well as smaller), and different
boards are allowed to have different board sizes.

* There are now "zones" which can be used like the old rotation maps but
have many more features.

* Stats are now separate from instances of stats. Stats cannot be added or
removed at run time, although instances can be created and destroyed (even
if there are initially no instances); a stat can have multiple instances.
Each stat has its own script which is shared by all instances of that stat
(although instances can have their own instruction pointers and lock flag).

* The global string table cannot be used for scripts; any board with
scripts will have them local to the board. (However, there is one global
script, and scripts can still be loaded from the global string table as
well as from script library lumps.)

* There are now 32 attribute bits instead of only 16.

* Some attribute bits are no longer present: Script (any stat can now have
a script), Stat (any element can be a stat or not, but which ones are stats
by default in the board editor are defined separately), Permanent (not
applicable, because board memory is no longer tied to screen layouts),
Transport (events are now used instead), Protected. There is a new bit
called "Permanent" but it works differently from ZZ Zero.

* The board now has three layers, each of which has four bytes per tile
instead of only two. Due to having multiple layers, it will not destroy
floors when non-stats move on them, and the under kind/colour of stats
is no longer needed (they are stored in the under layer instead).

* Appearance codes now work differently and are more versatile.

* The "printing variables" feature of ZZ Zero is not used but it is also
not necessary; screen layouts and numeric formats are more versatile.

* Many other limits have been increased.

There are many other enhancements compared with ZZ Zero, as well.


=== Comparison with OHRRPGCE ===

Like OHRRPGCE, Super ZZ Zero also uses the lumped Hamster archive format to
store everything that is a part of the game world in one file, rather than
needing several files like some other game engines do.

Unlike OHRRPGCE, the save game file will store the entire game state,
rather than only some things, and this cannot be customized.

Maps in Super ZZ Zero can be defined as persistent, even in save games;
in OHRRPGCE cannot persist if you leave and then enter them again (although
you can use NPC one-time-use tags, this feature is unnecessary in Super ZZ
Zero because of room persistence). Persistence can be enabled and disabled
individually per room, and also modified at run time.

Super ZZ Zero allows multiple inventory lists rather than only one, and
they can store additional information than only the item and quantity.
Inventory lists can also include additional information for the entire
list, such as maximum allowed total weight (item definitions can include
additional information such as weight), max items per slot (which can also
be defined per item definition as well), and a few others.


=== Comparison of file formats ===

-- Program ---------- Archive format ----- Structured data -----
   MegaZeux           ZIP archive          MZX Property List
   OHRRPGCE           Hamster archive      RELOAD
   Super ZZ Zero      Hamster archive      ASN.1X DER
   Weave ZZT          Separate files       N/A
   ZZ Zero            Hamster archive      N/A
   ZZT                N/A                  N/A
   ZZT Ultra          WAD                  JSON
   Zebra GCS          Separate files       Text format

The structured formats approximately from most compact to least compact
(the actual compactness may vary and is more complicated than shown here):

-- File format -------------- Min.node size --- Tags? --- Types? ---
   DER                                    2       [1]        Yes
   MegaZeux Property List                 6       Yes         No
   RELOAD                                 7       Yes        Yes
   JSON                                   ?       [2]        Yes
   XML                                    4       Yes         No
   ZXT                                   11       Yes         No

Note [1]: Tags are not required; if they are present, then they may be
implicit (meaning the type is not recorded) or explicit (in which case
the tag and type are both recorded).

Note [2]: Items in a key/value list can have keys, but this is not quite
the same as tags in other formats but can sometimes be used for a similar
purpose than tags in other formats.

Note that MegaZeux Property List is usually compressed because it is in
a ZIP archive file (although the world properties is not supposed to be
compressed, according to the documentation). This means that it usually
needs less disk space than uncompressed files, although the ZIP archive
format will store metadata which is not always needed for this purpose.

Hamster archive format does not have compression, and is concatenatable.
Each file only uses a header with the name (variable length) and length.

WAD uses fixed name lengths of eight bytes, and has a directory at the
beginning of the file instead, meaning offsets are included as well as
the lengths (even though some similar formats omit the lengths because
they can be calculated from the offsets).

In DER and JSON formats, each node has either a value or child nodes and
not both. In RELOAD and XML formats, each node can have both a value and
child nodes (RELOAD requires both but the value may be null and the number
of child nodes may be zero; XML also requires child nodes although there
is an abbreviation for a node that does not have any child nodes).

